From: h713 <h713@local>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 21:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] drm: h713-afbd: EXPERIMENT: the NV12 union test

Two experiments have attacked hardware YUV fetch on this display and each one
wrote half of the register set. Neither half worked, and the two nulls were
read as one conclusion. They are not.

  yuvtry, 2026-08-09/12/14      format selector 0x011 + all three strides +
                                AFBD_SRC. The Y/C plane addresses at 0x070/0x084
                                were left reading zero and dismissed at the time
                                as "a parallel mechanism we do not use".
                                Result: the fetch honoured the strides and
                                ignored the format -- the 4x horizontal repeat
                                is that arithmetic exactly, 1280 bytes per row
                                consumed as 320 ARGB pixels. Four runs, one
                                outcome, and explicitly NOT a shadowing or
                                sequencing problem: the selector read back 3
                                after the write and after the latch.

  patch 0065, 2026-08-25        format + plane strides + plane addresses +
                                dirty latch, and deliberately NOT AFBD_STRIDE or
                                AFBD_SRC, on the stated assumption that
                                "AFBD_SRC takes a single packed source and is
                                the RGB path". Result: every global register
                                landed correctly (Y=0x77100000, C=0x771E1000,
                                C-Y = 0xE1000 = one 1280x720 luma plane) and the
                                panel did not change, because the channel block
                                was still pointed at the fbcon framebuffer --
                                read back live during the same run as stride
                                0x1400 and source 0x76D00000.

So 0065's null is not evidence about YUV. It is evidence that a channel which
was never repointed keeps scanning what it was already scanning. The register
file it wrote is in AFBD *global* space, below the per-channel blocks: the
channel stride is 0x40, channel 0 starts at 0x100 and channel 1 at 0x140, so
0x011/0x040/0x044/0x06c/0x070/0x084 are common to the window and DO apply to
the live fetch path. 0065's conclusion that they drive "a SEPARATE video plane"
does not follow from its own data.

THIS PATCH WRITES BOTH HALVES IN ONE COMMIT, which nobody has done: the global
two-plane descriptor AND the channel block repointed at the luma plane.
Configure everything, publish with the dirty latch, then arm the channel --
"configure early, latch late", the same ordering note the external review made
about the plane experiments.

Two module parameters, because a hardware cycle on this board costs a build, a
flash, a boot and a photograph, and there are exactly two uncertain axes:

  chan_src=1     (default) write AFBD_STRIDE and AFBD_SRC from the luma plane.
                 chan_src=0 reproduces patch 0065 byte for byte, which makes
                 the A/B a module-parameter write rather than a rebuild.
  chan_stride=N  override the channel stride. Default 0 uses the luma pitch,
                 which is what yuvtry used. 5120 is worth a sweep: if the fetch
                 is still packed at 4 bytes/pixel, a correctly-pitched
                 greyscale distinguishes "wrong pitch" from "format inert".

WHAT THE OUTCOMES MEAN. All three are diagnostic, which is the point.

  correct colour bars     The route exists. Cedrus -> NV12 dma-buf -> display
                          -> panel with neither CPU nor GPU touching the pixels.
                          This is the no-GPU target.

  4x repeat / greyscale   The fetch is still packed RGB with both halves
                          written, so the format selector really is inert on
                          this channel and the "UI channel, and Allwinner UI
                          channels are RGB-only, YUV belongs to a VI channel"
                          reading stands. The route then needs DE/mixer
                          topology -- which arrives wholesale from the MIPS
                          firmware's LogoRegData replay -- and not register
                          pokes. Take the format back out and stop.

  no change at all        Neither half reached the fetch. Suspect the dirty
                          latch or the ordering before suspecting the
                          hypothesis; try chan_stride and the dirty-latch
                          position first.

THE PANEL IS THE VERDICT, NOT THE READBACK. Every one of the yuvtry runs
reported success at every register step -- format written, strides written,
latch written, commit OK -- while the glass said otherwise, and the *specific*
defect in the photograph is what identified the register each time. The
one-shot drm_info() dump here is for correlating a result, never for calling
one. Photograph the panel.

RISK. The RGB path is byte-for-byte as it was: the format selector is written
only on an actual format change, and h->format is seeded at probe from what
U-Boot leaves running, so an RGB-only session issues exactly the same register
writes as without this patch. A bad NV12 configuration produces garbage on the
projector, which a reboot clears -- yuvtry and 0065 both did this and both
restored cleanly. This driver never touches timing, the LVDS PHY or the display
reset, so it cannot strand the panel dark.

OUT OF SERIES, like 0054 and the DEBUG patches. Add it to series to run the
test, and remove it afterwards whatever the answer: on a negative it leaves a
format that negotiates and then displays nothing, which is a worse trap for the
next person than no NV12 support at all. That is why 0065 is out of series and
this inherits the same rule.

Compile-tested only (ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1, clean, no warnings). NOT RUN ON
HARDWARE at the time of writing.


=======================================================================
RUN ON HARDWARE 2026-08-25. NEGATIVE, AND DECISIVE. NOT IN series.
=======================================================================

The union works as a test and answers the question against us. Both halves
were confirmed in hardware simultaneously, live, mid-stream -- which is what
neither previous attempt could say:

    0x05600010 = 0x03000310    format byte 0x11 = 03, the NV12 row
    0x05600040 = 0x00000500    luma stride 1280
    0x05600044 = 0x00000500    chroma stride 1280
    0x05600070 = 0x77300000    Y plane
    0x05600084 = 0x773E1000    C plane   (C - Y = 0xE1000, one luma plane)
    0x05600170 = 0x00000500    channel stride 1280   <-- 0065 never wrote this
    0x05600178 = 0x77100000    channel source = luma <-- nor this
    0x05600140 = 0x03001901    ctrl unchanged, READY consumed every vsync

kmssink negotiated NV12 and streamed; the AFBD IRQ advanced throughout.

THE PANEL SHOWED THE 4x REPEAT AGAIN. Photographed in
local/lcd-photos/test_57/IMG_0728.MOV.

Then the chan_stride sweep, which is what makes this decisive rather than one
more null. Two settings, and a packed 4-bytes/pixel fetch predicts both
exactly:

  chan_stride=1280   720 display rows x 1280 bytes = 921600 bytes, which is
                     the luma plane precisely. So the whole screen is luma:
                     ALL GREYSCALE, 4x repeat. Observed: greyscale bars,
                     repeating 4 times.

  chan_stride=5120   Each display row now advances 5120 and consumes 5120, so
                     the buffer is walked contiguously. Luma (921600 bytes)
                     fills 921600/5120 = 180 rows; chroma (1280x360 = 460800)
                     fills 460800/5120 = 90 rows; past that is unrelated
                     memory. Predicts a luma band over a chroma band in a
                     STRICT 2:1 HEIGHT RATIO, then white.

                     Observed, measured off the photograph: greyscale band
                     ~115 px, green/magenta band ~60 px. Ratio 1.9:1 against a
                     required 2.0:1, hand-held, off-axis. The green/magenta is
                     interleaved UV read as packed BGRA -- that is what chroma
                     looks like through an RGB fetch.

So the channel stride at 0x170 IS honoured -- it moved the band boundaries
exactly where the arithmetic put them -- and the format selector at 0x011 is
NOT. The fetch stayed 4 bytes/pixel with byte 0x11 reading 03 the whole time,
and the hardware walked the NV12 buffer LINEARLY, as one packed RGB surface,
never as two planes. The plane addresses and plane strides changed nothing that
the channel registers did not already account for.

WHAT THIS SETTLES. The 2026-08-14 inference was right after all: this is a UI
channel, Allwinner UI channels are RGB-only, and YUV belongs to a VI channel.
0065's postmortem was wrong to call 0x011/0x040/0x044/0x06c/0x070/0x084 "a
SEPARATE video plane" -- they are global to the window and they do reach the
live path -- but its null and yuvtry's null were both, in the end, the same
fact seen from different sides.

Hardware YUV fetch on this display is not reachable by any register write to
the AFBD window. It needs the DE/mixer topology that decides channel type, and
that arrives wholesale from the MIPS firmware's LogoRegData replay. That is the
M4-scale work the 2026-08-14 note named, and this patch is the third and last
confirmation that pokes will not substitute for it.

Do not run a fourth variant of this experiment. The register space is now
covered: format alone (yuvtry), format + plane file (0065), and both plus the
channel block (here), across two stride regimes. There is nothing left to
permute.

The RGB path restored itself cleanly when the stream stopped -- format byte
back to 00, channel back to stride 5120 and the fbcon framebuffer, no oops --
so the restore logic is sound. It is out of series anyway: on this result it
would leave a format that negotiates and then displays garbage, which is a
worse trap for the next person than no NV12 support at all.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
@@ -67,9 +67,49 @@
 #define AFBD_STRIDE		0x170	/* bytes per line */
 #define AFBD_SRC		0x178	/* source physical address */
 
+/*
+ * AFBD *global* block, below the per-channel blocks at 0x100/0x140. These are
+ * not channel 0's registers -- the channel stride is 0x40, so channel 0 starts
+ * at 0x100 and everything here is common to the window. That matters, because
+ * it means these apply to the live fetch path rather than to the unserviced
+ * channel.
+ *
+ * Semantics recovered from the vendor's dec_reg_set_address() (base + 0x60,
+ * index 0 at +16 and +36) and dec_reg_set_dirty().
+ */
+#define AFBD_FORMAT		0x011	/* byte: fmt_attr_tbl row index */
+#define AFBD_PLANE_STRIDE0	0x040	/* luma bytes per line */
+#define AFBD_PLANE_STRIDE1	0x044	/* chroma bytes per line */
+#define AFBD_DIRTY		0x06c	/* write 1 to publish the config */
+#define AFBD_PLANE_ADDR0	0x070	/* Y plane physical address */
+#define AFBD_PLANE_ADDR1	0x084	/* C plane physical address */
+
+/*
+ * fmt_attr_tbl row indices, not DEC_FORMAT_* ids. Row 0 is what the live path
+ * U-Boot leaves running reads as; the vendor writes 6 and 7 for 10-bit YUV420
+ * and AV1, which puts 8-bit NV12 at row 3.
+ */
+#define AFBD_FMT_RGB888		0
+#define AFBD_FMT_NV12		3
+
 #define AFBD_IRQ_VSYNC		BIT(0)
 #define AFBD_STATUS_DONE	BIT(1)
 
+/*
+ * The variable actually under test. See the header comment on
+ * h713_afbd_set_nv12(): with chan_src=1 this is the union of the two previous
+ * experiments; with chan_src=0 it reproduces patch 0065 exactly.
+ */
+static bool chan_src = true;
+module_param(chan_src, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(chan_src,
+		 "NV12: also point the channel block (STRIDE/SRC) at the luma plane (default 1; 0 reproduces patch 0065)");
+
+static int chan_stride;
+module_param(chan_stride, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(chan_stride,
+		 "NV12: override the channel stride in bytes (default 0 = use the luma pitch)");
+
 struct h713_afbd {
 	struct drm_device drm;
 	struct drm_simple_display_pipe pipe;
@@ -79,6 +119,8 @@
 	struct clk *clk_bus;
 	struct drm_display_mode mode;
 	unsigned int stride;
+	u32 format;		/* last DRM fourcc programmed */
+	bool logged;		/* NV12 readback dumped once */
 };
 
 static inline struct h713_afbd *to_h713(struct drm_device *drm)
@@ -87,28 +129,140 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * Point AFBD at a buffer and latch it. Identical in order and content to
- * gles-play's commit path, minus its 50 ms poll for STATUS_DONE: here the
- * vblank interrupt reports completion, so nothing spins.
+ * Arm the channel commit. Identical in order and content to gles-play's commit
+ * path, minus its 50 ms poll for STATUS_DONE: here the vblank interrupt reports
+ * completion, so nothing spins.
  */
-static void h713_afbd_set_source(struct h713_afbd *h, dma_addr_t addr,
-				 unsigned int stride)
+static void h713_afbd_commit(struct h713_afbd *h)
 {
 	u32 pending;
 
+	pending = readl(h->regs + AFBD_STATUS);
+	if (pending)
+		writel(pending, h->regs + AFBD_STATUS);
+
+	writel(readl(h->regs + AFBD_CTRL) | 1u, h->regs + AFBD_CTRL);
+	writel(1, h->regs + AFBD_READY);
+}
+
+/*
+ * THE UNION TEST. Two earlier experiments each wrote half of this and each was
+ * inconclusive for a different reason:
+ *
+ *   yuvtry (2026-08-14)  format + all three strides + AFBD_SRC, but the Y/C
+ *                        plane addresses were left at zero. The fetch honoured
+ *                        the strides -- the "4x horizontal repeat" photograph
+ *                        is that arithmetic, 1280 bytes/row consumed as 320
+ *                        ARGB pixels -- and ignored the format. From that came
+ *                        "this is a UI channel and UI channels are RGB-only".
+ *
+ *   patch 0065 (08-25)   format + plane strides + plane addresses + dirty, but
+ *                        deliberately NOT AFBD_STRIDE/AFBD_SRC, on the stated
+ *                        assumption that "AFBD_SRC is the RGB path". So the
+ *                        channel block still pointed at the fbcon framebuffer
+ *                        (read back live: stride 0x1400, source 0x76D00000) and
+ *                        the panel never changed.
+ *
+ * Nobody has written both halves in one commit. This does: the global two-plane
+ * descriptor AND the channel block repointed at the luma plane. Configure
+ * everything first, publish, then latch -- the review's "configure early, latch
+ * late" note about the plane experiments applies here too.
+ *
+ * Three outcomes, all diagnostic:
+ *
+ *   correct colour bars     the route exists; this is the no-GPU path
+ *   4x repeat / greyscale   fetch is still packed RGB, the format selector is
+ *                           inert on this channel, and the UI-vs-VI channel-type
+ *                           reading stands. Route needs DE topology, not pokes.
+ *   no change at all        neither half reached the fetch; suspect the dirty
+ *                           latch or the ordering, not the hypothesis.
+ *
+ * The panel is the verdict, not the readback. Every one of the yuvtry runs
+ * reported success at every register step while the glass said otherwise.
+ */
+static void h713_afbd_set_nv12(struct h713_afbd *h,
+			       struct drm_plane_state *state)
+{
+	struct drm_framebuffer *fb = state->fb;
+	dma_addr_t y = drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(fb, state, 0);
+	dma_addr_t c = drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(fb, state, 1);
+	unsigned int stride = chan_stride ? chan_stride : fb->pitches[0];
+
+	if (h->format != DRM_FORMAT_NV12) {
+		writeb(AFBD_FMT_NV12, h->regs + AFBD_FORMAT);
+		h->format = DRM_FORMAT_NV12;
+		/* Force the RGB stride to be rewritten on the way back. */
+		h->stride = 0;
+	}
+
+	writel(fb->pitches[0], h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_STRIDE0);
+	writel(fb->pitches[1], h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_STRIDE1);
+	writel(lower_32_bits(y), h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_ADDR0);
+	writel(lower_32_bits(c), h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_ADDR1);
+
+	/*
+	 * The half patch 0065 left out. Without this the channel keeps
+	 * scanning whatever it scanned before, which is exactly what happened.
+	 */
+	if (chan_src) {
+		writel(stride, h->regs + AFBD_STRIDE);
+		writel(lower_32_bits(y), h->regs + AFBD_SRC);
+		h->stride = stride;
+	}
+
+	writel(1, h->regs + AFBD_DIRTY);
+
+	if (!h->logged) {
+		h->logged = true;
+		drm_info(&h->drm,
+			 "NV12 union: fmt=%02x pstride=%u/%u Y=%08x C=%08x chan_src=%d stride=%u src=%08x dirty=%u\n",
+			 readb(h->regs + AFBD_FORMAT),
+			 readl(h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_STRIDE0),
+			 readl(h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_STRIDE1),
+			 readl(h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_ADDR0),
+			 readl(h->regs + AFBD_PLANE_ADDR1),
+			 chan_src,
+			 readl(h->regs + AFBD_STRIDE),
+			 readl(h->regs + AFBD_SRC),
+			 readl(h->regs + AFBD_DIRTY));
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * The packed RGB path, byte-for-byte as it was. The format selector is only
+ * touched when coming back from NV12: U-Boot leaves it on the RGB row and
+ * h->format is seeded at probe to match, so an RGB-only session issues exactly
+ * the same register writes as before this patch.
+ */
+static void h713_afbd_set_rgb(struct h713_afbd *h,
+			      struct drm_plane_state *state)
+{
+	struct drm_framebuffer *fb = state->fb;
+	unsigned int stride = fb->pitches[0];
+
+	if (h->format != fb->format->format) {
+		writeb(AFBD_FMT_RGB888, h->regs + AFBD_FORMAT);
+		h->format = fb->format->format;
+	}
+
 	if (stride != h->stride) {
 		writel(stride, h->regs + AFBD_STRIDE);
 		h->stride = stride;
 	}
 
-	writel(lower_32_bits(addr), h->regs + AFBD_SRC);
+	writel(lower_32_bits(drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(fb, state, 0)),
+	       h->regs + AFBD_SRC);
+}
 
-	pending = readl(h->regs + AFBD_STATUS);
-	if (pending)
-		writel(pending, h->regs + AFBD_STATUS);
+static void h713_afbd_set_source(struct h713_afbd *h,
+				 struct drm_plane_state *state)
+{
+	if (state->fb->format->format == DRM_FORMAT_NV12)
+		h713_afbd_set_nv12(h, state);
+	else
+		h713_afbd_set_rgb(h, state);
 
-	writel(readl(h->regs + AFBD_CTRL) | 1u, h->regs + AFBD_CTRL);
-	writel(1, h->regs + AFBD_READY);
+	h713_afbd_commit(h);
 }
 
 static void h713_afbd_pipe_enable(struct drm_simple_display_pipe *pipe,
@@ -122,10 +276,7 @@
 	 * buffer the client handed us so the first commit is not a no-op.
 	 */
 	if (plane_state->fb)
-		h713_afbd_set_source(h,
-				     drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(plane_state->fb,
-							     plane_state, 0),
-				     plane_state->fb->pitches[0]);
+		h713_afbd_set_source(h, plane_state);
 
 	drm_crtc_vblank_on(&pipe->crtc);
 }
@@ -150,9 +301,7 @@
 	unsigned long flags;
 
 	if (state->fb)
-		h713_afbd_set_source(h,
-				     drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(state->fb, state, 0),
-				     state->fb->pitches[0]);
+		h713_afbd_set_source(h, state);
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&crtc->dev->event_lock, flags);
 	event = crtc->state->event;
@@ -256,6 +405,13 @@
 static const u32 h713_afbd_formats[] = {
 	DRM_FORMAT_XRGB8888,
 	DRM_FORMAT_ARGB8888,
+	/*
+	 * Advertised so a client will hand us two-plane YUV at all. This is an
+	 * experiment: if the union test below comes back negative this format
+	 * must come straight back out, because one that negotiates and then
+	 * displays nothing is a worse trap than no support.
+	 */
+	DRM_FORMAT_NV12,
 };
 
 /*
@@ -280,8 +436,14 @@
 	}
 
 	h->stride = readl(h->regs + AFBD_STRIDE);
-	drm_info(drm, "adopting %ux%u, stride %u, source %08x\n",
-		 w, hgt, h->stride, readl(h->regs + AFBD_SRC));
+	/*
+	 * U-Boot leaves the packed RGB path running; match it so the first RGB
+	 * commit does not rewrite a selector that is already correct.
+	 */
+	h->format = DRM_FORMAT_XRGB8888;
+	drm_info(drm, "adopting %ux%u, stride %u, source %08x, format %02x\n",
+		 w, hgt, h->stride, readl(h->regs + AFBD_SRC),
+		 readb(h->regs + AFBD_FORMAT));
 
 	cvt = drm_cvt_mode(drm, w, hgt, 60, false, false, false);
 	if (!cvt)
